{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC renderer robustness & performance hardening\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe DC path shells out to pandoc per field. Three hazards from the DC experiment: (1) per-field subprocess tax — a flat ~0.3s/field spawn cost regardless of\nbody size; a serial reconcile blocked ~20 min of wall time on `fork+exec`; (2) pathological-input hang — one corpus body drove pandoc's jira reader to spin\n13.5 min at 95.8% CPU, and pypandoc sets no timeout, so one field can stall the\nreconcile indefinitely; (3) pandoc is GPL and rides in the `pypandoc-binary` wheel, so its provenance and\nlicense must be pinned and recorded.\n\n## Prior art / dependency boundaries (do NOT re-decide 271c's choices)\n- **The renderer and its pandoc delivery are owned by 271c.** Story\n  `[rebar:271c-e88f-3220-4c9b]` (`repressive-delectable-swan`) LANDED the pure renderer\n  at `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` (merged `cf5155e528`), with pandoc from the\n  pinned **`pypandoc-binary==1.15` wheel** via a `wiki` extra, so the DC path needs no\n  system pandoc. This story hardens THAT module and pins THAT wheel; it adds no system\n  pandoc and no `acli`-style version+sha binary pin.\n- 271c leaves `WikiTextCodec` identity and `wiki_render.py` unreferenced by any codec op\n  or live send path; all live wiring is the cutover story\n  `[rebar:3388-96e8-e14e-4221]`'s and is **out of scope here**.\n- The change-gated \"no-op → no pandoc spawn\" property is that cutover story's\n  `rich_sha` gate, not on `origin/main` and not this story's to add. THIS story owns\n  only cost, timeout, fallback, batching and compliance of a render that IS performed.\n\n## Approach\n- **Batch contiguous SAFE units to bound per-render subprocess cost.** Verified against\n  the LANDED code (merged `cf5155e528`), not 271c's prose: `_lock_and_split`\n  (`wiki_render.py:150`) returns `(kind, text)` pairs whose kinds are the module\n  constants `_EXACT`/`_TABLE`/`_RENDER`/`_BLANK` (`:128-131`). This story groups each\n  **maximal run of contiguous `_RENDER` units** into ONE pandoc document → one wiki\n  segment, so many safe blocks cost one spawn per run, not one per unit. **A run\n  ABSORBS interleaved `_BLANK` units**, re-emitting their exact text as the blank lines\n  between blocks inside that document; a run breaks ONLY at a `_TABLE`/`_EXACT` unit.\n  Load-bearing: the landed splitter emits a `_BLANK` between every pair of blocks\n  (measured on `cf5155e528`: `render, blank, render, blank, render, blank, table, …`),\n  so if blanks broke a run, no run of length >1 could exist and the batching would be a\n  silent no-op. **No per-unit re-splitting of a batch's output**: the combined wiki is\n  one contiguous segment; reassembly is at the run level. (The pinned wheel's pandoc\n  **3.6.1** exposes no `--server`/daemon — verified absent from `--help` — so a\n  persistent server is out of scope.)\n- **Dangerous units are spliced VERBATIM — pandoc is never invoked on them.** A\n  dangerous unit corrupts pandoc's jira reader, so it passes through unchanged at its\n  original position. Order is restored by concatenating the ordered segments — no\n  delimiter splitting, since each invocation renders one contiguous safe run.\n- **Hard timeout + process-group reaping + batch-level verbatim fallback:** a plain\n  `subprocess.run(timeout=)` reaps only the DIRECT child, so a pandoc jira-reader\n  spinning at ~95.8% CPU (a pipe-holding grandchild) survives (bug d843, bpo-30154).\n  So the renderer MUST NOT use pypandoc's high-level `convert_text`/`convert_file` API\n  (no process handle); it locates pandoc via `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()` and builds\n  its OWN\n  `subprocess.Popen([pandoc_path, …], start_new_session=True)`, **enforces the\n  wall-clock timeout caller-side via `proc.communicate(timeout=dc_pandoc_timeout_s)`\n  and, on `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, passes that `Popen`** to the leaf\n  **`rebar._proc.reap_process_group`** (`_proc.py:26`) —\n  `killpg` SIGTERM→grace→SIGKILL→bounded drain, already reused by the ACLI transport\n  and grounding harness — killing the whole group, not just the direct child. It\n  kills/drains only AFTER a timeout is detected, so caller-side detection is required\n  (the `acli_subprocess.py` pattern). **The timeout is a config key**\n  (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default **10s**): >30× the observed ~0.3s/field\n  render and ~80× below the 13.5-min hang; `grace`/`drain` reuse the ACLI caller's\n  constants. On a safe-run call's timeout or non-zero exit, degrade **that entire\n  segment** to its original Markdown verbatim (echo-safe) and record a **segment-level\n  fallback marker**. The landed per-unit preservation check\n  (`_render_unit`/`code_fragments`, `wiki_render.py:376-390`, `:266-288`) is LIFTED to\n  the run: the check runs over the run's combined source vs its combined wiki, and a\n  missing fragment degrades that whole run — the same boundary as a timeout or non-zero\n  exit, including when pandoc exits 0. So segment boundary == fallback boundary for all\n  three failure modes, and no unit inside a run can be silently corrupted; other\n  segments and the verbatim dangerous units are unaffected.\n  The fallback boundary is exactly the pandoc-invocation boundary.\n- **Compliance:** pin the wheel, record pandoc's GPL license in the SBOM/notice, verify\n  the pin in CI.\n\n## Scope\n- `adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py` — own `subprocess.Popen(start_new_session=True)`\n  over `pypandoc.get_pandoc_path()`, per-invocation timeout +\n  `rebar._proc.reap_process_group`, safe-run batching, verbatim dangerous units,\n  run-level preservation check and fallback (all as above).\n- `pyproject.toml` — pin the `pypandoc-binary` wheel version (extends 271c's pin).\n- config (`reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default 10s). A `reconciler.*` key has a\n  fixed multi-file surface (mirrors `reconciler.jira_cli_timeout`); update together:\n  `_config_schema.py` — field `dc_pandoc_timeout_s: float = 10.0` on `ReconcilerConfig`\n  + validator `_as_float(v, k, minimum=0.0)`; `_config_sources.py` — a\n  `_CANONICAL_ENV_NAMES` alias only if a non-derived env name is wanted (else\n  auto-derived `REBAR_RECONCILER_DC_PANDOC_TIMEOUT_S`);\n  `tests/golden/config_surface.json` — deliberately REGENERATE (the guard\n  `tests/unit/test_config_surface_golden.py` is removal-only, so it will NOT flag a new\n  key's omission); `docs/config.md` — document it under `[tool.rebar.reconciler]`. A\n  unit test mirrors `test_config_file_wiring.py`'s four-consumer pattern.\n- CI workflow — verify the wheel pin (NOT an acli-style sha check).\n- SBOM/licensing notice — record the wheel-bundled pandoc GPL entry.\n- `tests/` — timeout, fallback, and batching tests over the vendored DC corpus.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every pandoc invocation runs under a caller-side wall-clock timeout via\n  `proc.communicate(timeout=dc_pandoc_timeout_s)` (config\n  `reconciler.dc_pandoc_timeout_s`, default 10s — above the ~0.3s normal render, below\n  the 13.5-min hang) and, on `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, process-group reaping via\n  `rebar._proc.reap_process_group` (the CPU-spinning grandchild is killed,\n  not orphaned); on timeout or non-zero exit it degrades that safe-run segment\n  to verbatim Markdown without raising and the render returns; verified by\n  `test_dc_pandoc_timeout_falls_back` (asserts the group is reaped) and\n  `test_dc_pandoc_error_degrades`.\n- [ ] A changed body of N contiguous safe units renders in one pandoc invocation (one\n  wiki segment) with original block order preserved, dangerous units are spliced\n  VERBATIM with no pandoc call, and a safe-run timeout degrades exactly that run's\n  segment (segment boundary == fallback boundary); verified by\n  `test_dc_render_batches_safe_run`, `test_dc_dangerous_unit_verbatim_no_pandoc`, and\n  `test_dc_segment_fallback_boundary`.\n- [ ] The pandoc binary comes from the `pypandoc-binary` wheel pinned by version in\n  `pyproject.toml` (271c's mechanism, not a system pandoc), the pin is verified in CI,\n  and its GPL license is recorded in the SBOM/notice; verified by the CI wheel-pin\n  check and a notice-file test."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786595112912292001,"uuid":"f6c82389-223a-429e-86d4-7021bdffe98c"}